WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:04.960
There are lots of good reasons
to try and follow a healthier diet—

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:07.040
you lose weight,
you feel good,

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.840
but the main reason?

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:13.360
To live a longer, happier,
more productive life.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.440
Welcome to the
NutritionFacts Podcast.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:18.240
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.920
Today we look at how to make
sense of the disparate results

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:26.040
from the four primate studies
on caloric restriction and lifespan.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:29.360
Calorie restriction
in primates to extend lifespan.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:30.600
Will it work?

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.520
There's only one way
to find out.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.040
There have been
four investigations

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:38.880
of calorie restriction and
lifespan in nonhuman primates.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.800
The first was published in 2003,

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:45.200
an analysis of the mortality
of 117 rhesus monkeys

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.640
followed for about 25 years
in a lab,

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:51.680
eight of whom had their
Purina monkey chow restricted.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:54.160
The average survival
of the restricted monkeys

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:56.880
was to 32 years of age,

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.920
compared to 25 years
for the control monkeys.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.880
However, it was more of
an observational study,

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:05.800
since the monkeys weren't
randomly assigned,

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:06.960
and although in the abstract

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.760
they talk about
the survival advantage

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:12.560
and how the ad libitum monkeys,
the eat-all-you-want monkeys,

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.320
had more than twice
the risk of death,

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:17.200
they acknowledge
deeper in the paper

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.640
that the difference in death

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:21.560
did not reach
statistical significance,

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:25.240
meaning it may very well
have been a fluke.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:26.880
That was all we had, though,

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.520
until results
started trickling in

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:31.720
from the famous pair of studies
that involved randomizing

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:34.720
about 200 rhesus monkeys
to caloric restriction

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.760
or more normal diets,

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.280
one out of the University
of Wisconsin-Madison

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.480
and another from the
National Institute of Aging.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.880
The UW study reported a 30
percent caloric restriction

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:49.600
significantly delayed disease
and improved survival,

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.360
but the NIA study did not.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:54.920
In the UW study,

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:56.800
caloric restriction
reduced the incidence

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.480
of age-related diseases
including cancer,

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:02.360
cardiovascular disease,
brain atrophy,

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:03.760
and muscle wasting,

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:05.520
preserving muscle mass,

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.480
demonstrating caloric
restriction can delay aging,

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:10.040
but in the NIA study,

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:11.920
the difference
in age-related diseases

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:15.200
did not achieve
statistical significance.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:16.760
In the UW study,

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:20.040
the restricted monkeys lived
to an average of about 29 years

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:21.760
compared to the control monkeys,

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:24.440
who lived closer to the average
for monkeys in captivity

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:26.680
of about 26 years old.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:29.320
In contrast, though one
of the restricted monkeys

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:32.920
in the NIA study became
the longevity record holder

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:35.080
for the species at age 43,

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:39.320
on average, the restricted group
didn't live any longer

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:41.360
than the control animals.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:45.480
Why the disparate results
between the two studies?

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:49.880
In the NIA study that found no
significant lifespan difference,

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:52.600
the control group
was not fed ad libitum,

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:55.440
but rather had food
portioned out to prevent

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:56.720
excessive weight gain.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.680
In contrast,
in the UW study,

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:01.040
the control monkeys could eat
as much as they wanted

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:02.000
throughout the day

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:07.040
so ended up weighing more
than the NIA control animals.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:09.520
Of course caloric restriction
would improve

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:12.440
the health and survival
of overweight monkeys

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:14.200
just as weight loss
would be beneficial

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.320
for overweight people,

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:18.840
but the null results
in the NIA study suggest

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:21.280
that normal weight people
might not benefit

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:23.360
from restricting further.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:27.200
The NIA monkeys were also fed
a healthier diet.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:28.840
The diet in the UW study

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:32.000
where they saw significant
benefit to cutting down

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.560
was an ultra-processed
concoction of largely

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:38.160
milk protein, corn oil,
corn starch, and table sugar,

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:41.640
whereas the NIA diet actually
included unprocessed plant foods

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:43.500
such as corn,
soybeans, and wheat,

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:46.760
and so actually had
some phytonutrients.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:51.120
About 29 percent of the UW diet
was straight sugar,

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:54.160
compared to 4 percent
in the NIA diet.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:57.400
American adults get about
17 teaspoons of sugar a day,

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:59.960
which is about
13 percent of calories.

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:03.600
The contrasting findings suggest
that the worse your diet is,

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:07.560
the more important it is
to eat less of it.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:10.440
The fourth study
was on grey mouse lemurs,

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:11.960
among the smallest of primates,

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:14.640
standing just three inches tall.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:17.480
Those randomized to
a 30 percent caloric restriction

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:19.560
compared to an ad libitum
control group

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.920
lived a whopping
50 percent longer.

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:26.760
And not just average lifespan,
but maximal lifespan.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:29.720
The maximum lifespan was boosted
by about 20 percent.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:33.280
However,
the "calorie restricted" group

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:37.080
still weighed heavier
than their wild counterparts,

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:40.000
so again this may just be
an illustration

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:41.840
of the harms of obesity

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:46.120
and another indictment against
all-you-can-eat buffets.

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:48.920
Also, those in the calorie
restriction group experienced

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:52.320
an acceleration of age-related
loss of grey matter

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:53.520
throughout their brains,

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:56.200
though this did not appear
to translate into cognitive

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:58.640
or behavioral differences.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:02.040
Pooling the three rhesus monkey
studies together,

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:05.760
there seemed to be
lower age-related mortality

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:09.560
but no significant difference
in average lifespans overall

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:11.600
between the caloric
restriction groups

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:13.360
and the control groups.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:14.680
Given the time and expense,

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:16.680
there is little chance
we are going to see any more

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:18.000
long-term primate studies

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.720
on caloric restriction
for life extension.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:24.240
So what can we draw
from the primate data to date?

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:27.200
If you're overweight
or living off junk food,

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:30.974
eating less is a good idea.

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:32.360
In our next story,

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:37.264
we discover how a slower metabolism
may actually be a good thing.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:40.560
We’ve known for more than a
century that calorie restriction

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:42.960
can increase the lifespan of animals,

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:45.960
and the metabolic slowdown
may be the mechanism.

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:50.840
That could be why the tortoise
lives 10 times longer than the hare.

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:55.200
Rabbits can live 10 to 20 years,
whereas “Harriet,” a tortoise

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:57.840
evidently collected from the
Galapagos by none other than

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:04.360
Charles Darwin himself in
the 1830s lived until 2006.

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:07.600
Slow and steady may win the race.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:10.000
One of the ways your body lowers
your resting metabolic rate

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:13.520
is by creating cleaner-burning,
more efficient mitochondria,

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:15.680
the power plants that fuel our cells.

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:20.240
It’s like your body passes its
own fuel-efficiency standards.

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:24.480
These new mitochondria create
the same energy with less oxygen

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:27.720
and produce less free radical “exhaust.”

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:30.680
After all, your body is
afraid famine is afoot;

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:34.640
and so, it is trying to conserve
as much energy as it can.

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:37.040
The largest caloric
restriction trial to date

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:41.480
indeed found both metabolic slowing
and a reduction in free radical-induced

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:46.360
oxidative stress, both of which
may slow the rate of aging.

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:51.680
The flame that burns twice
as bright burns half as long.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:54.600
But whether this will result
in greater human longevity

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:56.840
is an unanswered question.

00:06:56.840 --> 00:06:59.600
Caloric restriction is
often said to extend

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:02.760
the lifespan of “every species studied”.

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:07.760
But that isn’t even true of all
strains within a single species.

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:10.280
Some scientists don’t
think calorie restriction

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:12.440
will improve human longevity at all;

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:16.520
others suggest a 20 percent calorie
restriction starting at age 25

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:21.000
and sustained for 52 years could
add 5 years onto your life.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:24.160
Either way, the reduced oxidative stress

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:28.320
would be expected to
improve our health span.

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:31.320
Members of the
Calorie Restriction Society,

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:36.360
self-styled CRONies (for Calorie-
Restricted Optimal Nutrition),

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:40.600
appear to be in excellent health,
but they’re a rather unique

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:42.800
self-selected bunch of individuals.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:47.720
You don’t really know until
you put it to the test.

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:50.040
Enter the CALERIE study,

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:52.240
the Comprehensive
Assessment of Long-term

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:54.760
Effects of Reducing Intake of Energy,

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:59.560
the first clinical trial to test
the effects of caloric restriction.

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:02.080
Hundreds of non-obese men
and women were randomized

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:05.560
to two years of 25% calorie restriction.

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:09.480
They only ended up achieving half
that but lost about 18 pounds

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:11.480
and three inches off their waists,

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:15.760
wiping out more than half of
their visceral abdominal fat.

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:19.680
That translated into significant
improvements in cholesterol levels,

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:22.920
triglycerides, insulin
sensitivity, and blood pressures.

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:25.360
Eighty percent of those who
were overweight when they started

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:29.280
were normal weight by the end,
compared to a 27-percent increase

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:33.000
in those who became overweight
in the control group.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:37.120
In the famous Minnesota Starvation
Study that used conscientious objectors

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:41.520
as guinea pigs during World War II,
the study subjects suffered both

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:45.280
physically and psychologically,
experiencing depression,

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:47.240
irritability, and loss of libido.

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:52.800
The subjects started out lean, though,
and had their calorie intake cut in half.

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:56.400
The CALERIE study ended up
being 4 times less restrictive,

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:59.200
only about 12 percent below
baseline calorie intake,

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:04.520
and enrolled normal weight individuals,
which in the U.S. these days means

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:06.400
overweight on average.

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:09.000
As such, the CALERIE subjects
experienced nothing

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:12.920
but positive quality-of-life benefits,
with significant improvements in

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:16.480
mood, general health,
sex drive, and sleep.

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:20.000
They only ended up eating
about 300 fewer calories

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:22.160
than they were eating at baseline.

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:25.400
So, they got all these benefits—
the physiological benefits,

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:28.560
the psychological benefits—
all from only cutting about

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:34.120
a snack-sized bag of chips worth
of calories from their daily diets.

00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:36.880
What happened at the end
of the trial, though?

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:41.320
In the Minnesota Starvation Study
and calorie deprivation experiments

00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:45.880
done on Army Rangers, as soon as
subjects were released from restriction

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:50.600
they tended to rapidly regain
the weight and sometimes even more.

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:53.720
The leaner they started out,
the more their bodies seemed to

00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:57.520
drive them to overeat to pack
back on the extra body fat.

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:01.360
In contrast, after the
completion of the CALERIE study,

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:03.800
even though their
metabolism was slowed,

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:08.080
they retained about 50 percent of
the weight loss two years later.

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:10.800
They must have acquired new
eating attitudes and behaviors

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:13.360
that allowed them to
keep their weight down.

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:15.920
After extended calorie
restriction, for example,

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:21.975
cravings for sugary, fatty, and
junky foods may actually go down.

